The float32 network matches QDA on every training example (91.55%). Checking with repeated 8-fold CV whether very small covariance shrinkage helps unseen accuracy; I expect plain QDA to stay best or nearly so.
```python
exec(open("h.py").read())
res={}
for seed in range(4):
    r=np.random.default_rng(seed); ix=r.permutation(len(y)); fs=np.array_split(ix,8)
    for reg in [0,1e-5,1e-4,3e-4,1e-3,3e-3]:
        for f in fs:
            tr=np.setdiff1d(ix,f); mm=qda_fit(X[tr],y[tr],reg); res.setdefault(reg,[]).append((qda_pred(mm,X[f])==y[f]).mean())
for k,v in res.items(): print(k,round(np.mean(v),4))
